// EXPECT: PASS
// Regression test for a real bug found by AFL++ fuzzing this session (see
// cpp/ToDo): pegexp_lookahead() (adlparser.h) failed to write its advanced
// scan position back to its caller, so a pegexp literal containing a
// lookahead ('&'/'!') could never be scanned past - an unterminated one
// (e.g. "/&0" with no closing '/') hung forever. Fixed, and confirmed this
// doesn't just avoid the hang but actually works: strpp's own pegexp.h
// already correctly implements lookahead as a zero-width assertion at
// match time (see strpp/test/pegexp_test.cases) - this only needed the
// ADL-side *source-text* scanner (finding where "/regex/" ends in the
// .adl file) to correctly advance through a lookahead's own characters,
// which is unrelated to - and doesn't compromise - that zero-width match
// semantics (see pegexp.h's own skip_atom() for the same distinction).
//
// Like every other test here, run_tests.sh only checks the exit code, so
// this confirms the file parses without crashing/hanging, not that the
// match result is exactly "y" - that was checked by hand against the
// trace, not asserted by this test.
TOP {
	NotX: {
		Syntax = /!(x)./
	}
	Good: NotX = y;			// Doesn't start with 'x': lookahead passes, matches
}
